**How each security goal is met:**

| Goal | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| **Cannot forge / guess / alter** | Token = `base64url(JSON payload) . base64url(HMAC-SHA-256(payload, secret))`. Any change to the payload invalidates the signature. A handler with a different `secret` produces a different HMAC and rejects the token. |
| **1-hour expiry** | Payload carries `iat` (login epoch-ms). `verifyToken` rejects when `now() ≥ iat + 3 600 000`. |
| **Logout revocation** | Each token has a unique random `jti` (16 bytes from `crypto.getRandomValues`). `POST /logout` adds that `jti` to an in-memory `Set`; subsequent verification looks it up. Other tokens (different `jti`) are unaffected. |
| **Members cannot reach admin** | `GET /admin/stats` checks `auth.role !== "admin"` and returns **403**. |
| **No username oracle** | Both "user not found" and "wrong password" return the identical `401 { "error":"invalid_credentials" }` response. |
| **No password / secret leakage in token** | The payload contains only `sub`, `iat`, `jti`. The HMAC is a one-way digest; it reveals neither the password nor the secret. |
| **Uniqueness per login** | The 128-bit random `jti` (plus the timestamp) makes two tokens for the same user at the same instant differ with overwhelming probability. |

